# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .gitattributes — how git should treat the bytes in these files.
#
# WHY: `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine git setting, commonly `true` on Windows,
# meaning "store LF in the repository but write CRLF into my working directory".
# It is not shared between clones, so every contributor and CI runner may have it
# set differently. Two failures follow, neither of which looks like a line-ending
# problem when you hit it:
#
#   1. Phantom diffs — change one line, and `git diff` shows the whole file as
#      modified because every ending was rewritten. The real change becomes
#      unreviewable, which matters most in exactly the files you want reviewed
#      carefully, like a security guard.
#
#   2. Text the tools read. `fs_tools.read_file` returns content that is then split
#      on "\n". With CRLF checkouts, every line carries a trailing "\r", so
#      `search_in_file` context strings gain stray characters and any future exact
#      match against a line becomes subtly wrong. Normalised endings mean the
#      fixtures behave the same on every machine.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` tells git: treat these as text, normalise to LF on commit,
# and check them out as LF regardless of the local `core.autocrlf`.
#
# WHY HERE RATHER THAN A GIT SETTING: .gitattributes is committed, so it applies
# to everyone who clones, including CI. `git config` fixes exactly one machine and
# has to be remembered again on the next one. Configuration that must hold for the
# project belongs in the project.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary, so git never attempts line-ending conversion. Rewriting a
# byte inside a PDF that happened to look like a CRLF corrupts the file.
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.pyc binary

# Jupyter notebooks are JSON with embedded output. Marking them as text is right,
# but they are worth flagging: the diffs are enormous and largely noise, because
# execution counts and cell output change on every run even when no code did.
*.ipynb text eol=lf
